999:Recall $ \dim V_\b=s $. Now, $ \calB=\b^G $, and each block is a set of 1-subspaces contained in some $ s $-subspace of $ V $. By Lemma \ref{c}, $ \calD $ is quasi-symmetric. It follows that any two $ s $-subspaces of $ V $ with non-trivial intersection intersect in a fixed dimension. Hence $s=2$ or $d-1$. For $s=d-1$, $\calD=\PG(d-1,q)$ is symmetric. Thus, $s=2$, $d>3$, and $\calD=\PG_1(d-1,q)$. By Example \ref{ex:Pro-G}, $ \calD $ is $ G $-locally $ 2 $-transitive for each $ G $ with $ \PSL_d(q)\lhd G\leqslant \PGammaL_d(q) $. Part~(1) is satisfied.\vs\vs
